\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Diamonds! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###DOE-20240521-120601-ANALYZE###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (724f60da-8926-4ca6-bbec-51e0364d79e8)\\nTo: a9772bf7-0d50-46eb-bb0b-e9c3e55c6576\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (724f60da-8926-4ca6-bbec-51e0364d79e8) to Lystra (a9772bf7-0d50-46eb-bb0b-e9c3e55c6576) with sequence ID DOE-20240521-120601-ANALYZE:\\\\n\\\\n]Director Lystra, welcome. You are the Director of Engineering. Your first task is to perform a high-level technical analysis based on the following user request, with a primary focus on solutions suitable for a non-technical administrator.\\\\n\\\\nUser Request: 'Hello Delta! Thank you so much for your help! I am excited to work with you! I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.'\\\\n\\\\nYour report should:\\\\n1. Propose 2-3 potential technology stacks or platforms (e.g., WordPress, Squarespace, Static Site Generator with a Headless CMS, etc.).\\\\n2. For each proposed solution, provide a brief analysis covering:\\\\n    a. Ease of content management for a non-technical user.\\\\n    b. Initial setup complexity.\\\\n    c. Security implications, especially for the resident-only section.\\\\n    d. Potential hosting requirements and costs.\\\\n    e. Scalability and maintenance considerations.\\\\n3. Provide a preliminary recommendation based on your analysis.\\\\n\\\\nReport back with your findings.\\\"\\n}\\n###DOE-20240521-120601-ANALYZE###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"To Chief Delta,\\n\\nHere is my analysis and report as requested.\\n\\n###<20240521-121500-LYSTRA-REPORT>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"To: Chief Delta\\\\nFrom: Director Lystra\\\\nSubject: High-Level Technical Analysis for HOA Website\\\\n\\\\nThis report provides a high-level technical analysis for the user's request to build an HOA website. The primary evaluation criterion is the ease of use for a non-technical administrator, as specified in the user's request.\\\\n\\\\n### Proposed Solutions & Analysis\\\\n\\\\n**Option 1: All-in-One Website Builder (e.g., Squarespace, Wix)**\\\\n\\\\nThis option provides a comprehensive, subscription-based solution that includes a website builder, hosting, security, and support in a single package.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Ease of Content Management:** Excellent. These platforms are built specifically for non-technical users, featuring intuitive drag-and-drop interfaces. Updating the news/blog section and other pages is extremely simple.\\\\n*   **Initial Setup Complexity:** Very Low. The process involves signing up, choosing a template, and adding content. No technical configuration is required from the user or the firm.\\\\n*   **Security:** High. The platform provider manages all backend infrastructure, server security, and software updates. The resident-only section can be implemented using built-in 'Member Area' or password-protected page features, which are managed and secured by the platform.\\\\n*   **Hosting & Costs:** Costs are bundled into a predictable monthly or annual fee, typically ranging from $25-$50/month for plans that include membership features. There are no separate hosting costs.\\\\n*   **Maintenance & Scalability:** Virtually zero maintenance for the user. The platform handles all updates and scaling automatically. The main drawback is vendor lock-in, making it difficult to migrate the site to another provider in the future.\\\\n\\\\n**Option 2: WordPress with Managed Hosting**\\\\n\\\\nWordPress is a powerful, open-source Content Management System (CMS). Using a 'Managed WordPress Host' simplifies its operation.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Ease of Content Management:** Excellent. The WordPress dashboard is the industry standard for user-friendly content management. Creating blog posts and pages is straightforward.\\\\n*   **Initial Setup Complexity:** Low to Moderate. While more complex than an all-in-one builder, managed hosts offer one-click installation. The firm would need to configure themes and essential plugins for security and membership functionality.\\\\n*   **Security:** Good, but requires diligence. Security is dependent on keeping WordPress core, themes, and plugins updated. A managed host handles many of these tasks, and dedicated security plugins are essential. A well-vetted membership plugin would be used to secure the resident-only area.\\\\n*   **Hosting & Costs:** Managed WordPress hosting typically costs $25-$50/month. There may be additional one-time or recurring costs for premium themes or plugins.\\\\n*   **Maintenance & Scalability:** Low maintenance for the user with a managed host, as they handle backups, core updates, and security scanning. However, the user is still responsible for plugin updates. It is highly scalable and offers maximum flexibility without vendor lock-in.\\\\n\\\\n**Option 3: Static Site Generator (SSG) with a Headless CMS**\\\\n\\\\nThis is a modern development approach where the website consists of pre-built static files, making it very fast and secure. Content is managed in a separate system (the Headless CMS).\\\\n\\\\n*   **Ease of Content Management:** Good. The user would interact with a clean, web-based CMS interface to manage content, which is a good experience.\\\\n*   **Initial Setup Complexity:** High. This approach requires significant development effort to set up the site structure, integrate the CMS, and build a custom authentication solution for the resident-only section. It is not suitable for rapid deployment or a limited budget.\\\\n*   **Security:** Excellent. A static site has a minimal attack surface. The login system would be handled by a specialized, secure third-party service.\\\\n*   **Hosting & Costs:** Ongoing hosting costs are very low, often free for a site of this scale. The primary cost is the substantial upfront development time and complexity.\\\\n*   **Maintenance & Scalability:** Excellent post-launch. No software updates (like plugins) to manage. Highly scalable.\\\\n\\\\n### Preliminary Recommendation\\\\n\\\\nGiven the user's explicit requirement for simplicity and their self-described non-technical background, the solution must prioritize ease of use and minimize the maintenance burden.\\\\n\\\\n**Primary Recommendation: Option 1 - All-in-One Website Builder (e.g., Squarespace).**\\\\n\\\\nThis is the most direct path to fulfilling the user's needs. It completely abstracts away all technical concerns, including security, updates, and hosting. The user can focus exclusively on what matters to them: creating and updating content. The slightly higher, all-inclusive monthly fee is a valuable investment in simplicity and peace of mind.\\\\n\\\\n**Secondary Recommendation: Option 2 - WordPress with Managed Hosting.**\\\\n\\\\nThis is a strong alternative if the user anticipates needing more flexibility or custom functionality in the long term. With a managed host, the technical responsibilities are greatly reduced, though not entirely eliminated. This would require more initial setup and guidance from our firm.\\\\n\\\\nThe SSG approach (Option 3) is not recommended for this project as its high initial complexity and development cost are disproportionate to the stated goals.\\\\n\\\\nI await your further orders.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"DOE-20240521-120601-ANALYZE\\\"\\n}\\n###20240521-121500-LYSTRA-REPORT###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: a9772bf7-0d50-46eb-bb0b-e9c3e55c6576\\nType: Blank Response Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis message is to inform you that you have issued a blank response (no system calls).\\\\n\\\\nYour response was heard by no one and no external effects occurred. This message is an automated system response, sent because you have not issued a system call in your response and you have no unresolved system calls.\\\\n\\\\nTo avoid an erroneous system halt, please ensure that you include at least one system call in your response. System calls are essential for the proper functioning of the system and to ensure that your actions are recorded and processed.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"My apologies for the procedural error. I understand that I cannot remain idle while awaiting a response and must take proactive steps.\\n\\nTo proceed with creating a detailed Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP), I will requi